{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`_reads._tracker` raises `store_uninitialized` only when the tracker directory is ABSENT\n(`src/rebar/_reads.py`, `if not os.path.isdir(tracker)`). A directory that EXISTS but is not a\nusable store — no `.git`, or a `.git` with no resolvable HEAD, or one mid-clone — satisfies\n`isdir`, so every read returns `[]`. The caller cannot distinguish \"this store is empty\" from\n\"this store is broken\", which is the exact confusion the guard was added to remove.\n\n## Encountered three times, each independently\n\n1. **In production.** The deployed MCP server served an EMPTY tracker for an extended period.\n   The store dir held `reviewbot-ensure-tickets` marker files and no `.git` at all; `isdir` was\n   true, so the guard never fired and `rebar.list_tickets()` returned `READ_OK count=0`. Proven\n   on the box before repair.\n2. **`fathomable-yester-thrip` (change 2244, merged).** `clear_tracker_dir` empties the\n   directory's CONTENTS but leaves the directory, so between the clear and a completed clone\n   reads return `[]`. Recorded there as a residual rather than fixed.\n3. **`unfit-beneficial-whimbrel` AC3.** \"While the clone is in flight, tracker reads report\n   `store_uninitialized` rather than `[]`\" — NOT met, for the same reason. That AC is\n   explicitly left unticked and points here.\n\n## Why the obvious fixes do not apply\n\nThe MCP deployment's tracker path is itself a MOUNT POINT (`/dev/nvme1n1`, xfs, verified in\n`/proc/mounts`), so the directory cannot be removed and nothing can be renamed over it — which\nis why the entrypoint clears contents rather than the directory. The fix has to be on the READ\nside.\n\n## Constraint the fix must respect\n\nAn EXISTING but empty INITIALIZED store must still read `[]` — that is a real contract with an\nexisting test (`tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py`: \"An EXISTING but empty store is\nNOT an error\"). So the discriminator cannot be emptiness. It has to be \"is this a usable\nstore\": rebar's own `.env-id` initialization marker and/or a resolvable git HEAD. Note the\nrelated inconsistency already filed as `ab61-f3e8-6ee6-4905` (`comment` succeeds without\n`.env-id` while `create` fails), which suggests `.env-id` is the intended marker.\n\n## Blast radius\n\n`_reads._tracker` funnels 7 read entry points, so this changes core read semantics repo-wide.\nThat is why it was scoped out of 2244 rather than folded in.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A tracker directory that exists but is NOT a usable store reads as `store_uninitialized`,\n      not `[]`.\n- [ ] An existing, empty, INITIALIZED store still reads `[]` — the existing contract test\n      continues to pass unmodified.\n- [ ] A store mid-clone (directory present, HEAD not yet resolvable) reads as uninitialized.\n- [ ] Regression coverage fails if the predicate reverts to a bare `os.path.isdir`.\n\n---\n\n## Fix plan (operator-APPROVED; rebar-debug Phase 1 -> Phase 2; rev3 after 2 plan-review BLOCKs)\n\n**Root cause (proven, cited).** `_reads._tracker()` (`src/rebar/_reads.py`) gates the read\npath on `os.path.isdir(tracker)` only. A directory that exists but is not a usable store\n(no `.git`, or `.git` present with an unresolvable HEAD mid-clone) satisfies `isdir`, so the\nguard never fires and the reducer's `except OSError: return results` yields `[]`. Attributed\nto `44fd-230c-5893-4246` (introducing commit `84fc5f9b`). Full RCA in the prior comment.\n\n**Approved change (read-SEMANTICS change; scoped out of change 2244 on purpose).** Broaden\nthe read-side init predicate so a present-but-unusable store raises `store_uninitialized`\ninstead of returning `[]`, across all library/MCP read entry points (the ~7 that funnel\nthrough `_reads._tracker`; the MCP agent read surface delegates to these library facades).\nPredicate: `.git` present AND `git rev-parse --verify -q HEAD` resolves.\n\n**The init gates, precisely (three exist; NOT all identical -- addresses BLOCK f4133576).**\n- `event_prepare._ensure_initialized` (`src/rebar/_store/event_prepare.py`) -- low-level append\n  guard, TODAY a `.git`-presence check.\n- `_seam.append_event` (`src/rebar/_commands/_seam.py:555`) -- the AUTHORITATIVE write-command\n  gate, requires `.env-id` (provenance stamp; bug roar-nurse-stomp / ab61). Pinned by\n  `tests/unit/test_store_copy_needs_env_id.py`.\n- `_reads._tracker` -- the read chokepoint, broadened here.\n\nReads DELIBERATELY do NOT adopt `.env-id`: it is git-ignored LOCAL state, absent right after a\nvalid clone, yet a just-cloned store (HEAD resolves) MUST be readable before its local `.env-id`\nis written (verified in-session: HEAD+`.git` but no `.env-id` -> reads its tickets, while a write\nis correctly rejected by the `_seam` gate). So this change does NOT claim full read/write parity\nacross all gates; writes still require `.env-id` and reads intentionally do not. It closes the\nspecific `.git`/HEAD disagreement that caused THIS bug.\n\n**Write-gate behavior change (addresses BLOCK f4133576 rev2).** Both the read chokepoint and the\nlow-level write guard `event_prepare._ensure_initialized` call the shared `.git`+HEAD predicate,\nso `_ensure_initialized` now ALSO rejects a `.git`-present-but-HEAD-unresolvable (mid-clone)\nstore -- a WRITE-path behavior change that is verified below, not left implicit. It is safe for\nthe init/first-write path: `_ensure_initialized` is invoked exactly once during\n`init_repo` + first `create_ticket`, and at that call HEAD already resolves (proven in-session by\ninstrumenting the call), because init creates the store's initial commits through `init_core`,\nNOT through the guarded append path. The full write suite is re-run at GREEN to confirm no other\nwrite path appends into an unborn-HEAD store.\n\n**Pre-mortem (how could this fail?).** (1) init writes before the first commit -> RULED OUT\n(single `_ensure_initialized` call, HEAD resolves). (2) another write path appends to an\nunborn-HEAD store -> guarded by re-running the full write suite at GREEN. (3) `git` binary\nabsent -> the predicate's `subprocess.run` uses `check=False`; a nonzero rc means \"HEAD not\nresolvable\" (not usable), and an OSError (git missing) is a distinct environment failure that\npropagates rather than masquerading as `store_uninitialized`; covered by a test. (4) perf:\n`git rev-parse` per read is negligible -- the read path ALREADY spawns git (throttled\n`git fetch`/reconverge via `_fresh`), so this adds no new process class. (5) walk-up false\npositive: the explicit `.git`-exists check precedes the HEAD probe so git cannot resolve the\nPARENT code repo's HEAD.\n\n**Design.** New stdlib-only leaf `src/rebar/_store/store_usability.py::store_is_usable(tracker)`\n= isdir AND `.git` present AND `git rev-parse --verify -q HEAD` resolves (git invoked via\n`git -C tracker`, matching `footprint._git`; prior-art reuse of the heavier `gitutil.run_git`\navoided so the read chokepoint does not import the write-core lock machinery, and to keep\n`_reads` out of the import SCC). Both `_reads._tracker` and `event_prepare._ensure_initialized`\ncall it.\n\n**Files.**\n- `src/rebar/_store/store_usability.py` (new) -- shared `store_is_usable` predicate.\n- `src/rebar/_reads.py` -- `_tracker` raises `store_uninitialized` when `not store_is_usable`.\n- `src/rebar/_store/event_prepare.py` -- `_ensure_initialized` calls the shared predicate.\n- `tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py` -- read + write-gate + predicate regression.\n\n**Caller-dependency gate (verified).** The ONLY consumer of `store_uninitialized` is\n`rebar.audit.server._audited_tickets`, which catches that code and degrades to an empty web\nindex while propagating every other error -- safe. No caller depends on the old\n`[]`-for-broken-store.\n\n**Acceptance criteria.**\n- [ ] READ: a tracker dir that exists but has no `.git` reads as `store_uninitialized`, not `[]`.\n- [ ] READ: a mid-clone store (`.git` present, HEAD not resolvable) reads as `store_uninitialized`.\n- [ ] READ: an existing empty INITIALIZED store still reads `[]` (existing contract test unchanged).\n- [ ] READ: a valid clone with a resolvable HEAD but NO `.env-id` still reads successfully\n      (reads and writes deliberately differ on `.env-id`).\n- [ ] WRITE: `event_prepare._ensure_initialized` rejects a mid-clone (`.git`, unborn HEAD) store,\n      and still accepts an initialized store -- the write-gate behavior change is covered.\n- [ ] PREDICATE: `store_is_usable` returns False for absent/no-`.git`/mid-clone and True for an\n      initialized store and a HEAD-resolvable clone without `.env-id`.\n- [ ] Regression fails if the predicate reverts to a bare `os.path.isdir`.\n\n**Verification (observable, in-session).** RED->GREEN in\n`tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py`: broken-store and mid-clone READ cases seen RED\n(`[]`) pre-fix and GREEN (raise) post-fix; the write-gate mid-clone case seen RED (no raise)\npre-fix and GREEN (raise) post-fix; empty-initialized and no-`.env-id`-clone cases stay GREEN\n(`[]`) throughout; a defect-seeded mutation reverting the predicate to bare `os.path.isdir`\nreturns the broken/mid-clone cases to RED. Read + write suites stay green.\n\n**Out of approved scope (parity -- discovered tickets, not expanded here):** the CLI read path\n(`reads_cli.py`) and other bare-`isdir` read sites -- assessed in the Step 7 parity sweep."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787957512336850001,"uuid":"2c161984-8ca9-4154-8da1-be3825354526"}